%N A399694 Rank-2 Enots-Wolley sequence: the lexicographically earliest sequence of distinct positive integers beginning 1, 2 such that every later term has exactly two distinct prime factors, shares a prime factor with the previous term but none with the term two places back, and has a prime factor not dividing the previous term.
%C A399694 Let P(k) be the set of distinct prime divisors of k, and let omega(k) = |P(k)| = A001221(k). The restriction omega(k) = 2 concerns distinct prime factors only; arbitrary positive exponents are allowed. Thus, after the initial terms 1 and 2, all terms belong to A007774.
%C A399694 Equivalently, this is the rank-2, or biprimary, Enots-Wolley process EW[2]. It is obtained from the Enots-Wolley sequence A336957 by restricting every noninitial candidate to have exactly two distinct prime factors.
%C A399694 The sequence agrees with A336957 through a(29) = 91. The first difference is at n = 30: here a(30) = 56, whereas A336957 has a(30) = 42; the latter is excluded here because 42 has three distinct prime factors.
%F A399694 For n > 2, a(n) = min {m >= 1: m is not in {a(1),...,a(n-1)}, omega(m) = 2, P(m) intersect P(a(n-1)) is nonempty, P(m) intersect P(a(n-2)) is empty, and P(m) \ P(a(n-1)) is nonempty}.
%e A399694 a(3) = 6: its prime support is {2,3}; it shares the factor 2 with a(2) = 2, is coprime to a(1) = 1, and introduces the new prime 3.
%e A399694 For a(6), the two preceding terms are 15 and 35. A candidate must share a prime with 35, avoid both primes of 15, and introduce a prime not dividing 35. Thus it must retain 7, avoid 3 and 5, and introduce another prime. The least unused biprimary integer satisfying this is 14, so a(6) = 14.
%e A399694 At n = 30 the preceding terms are 65 = 5*13 and 91 = 7*13. A candidate must therefore contain 7, avoid 5 and 13, and contain another prime. The unrestricted Enots-Wolley candidate 42 = 2*3*7 is forbidden because omega(42) = 3; the least allowed unused candidate is 56 = 2^3*7. Hence a(30) = 56.
%o A399694 (Python)
%o A399694 from itertools import count
%o A399694 def P(n):
%o A399694  s=set();d=2
%o A399694  while d*d<=n:
%o A399694   if n%d:d+=1
%o A399694   else:s.add(d);n//=d
%o A399694  if n>1:s.add(n)
%o A399694  return s
%o A399694 def EW2(n):
%o A399694  a=[1,2]
%o A399694  while len(a)<n:
%o A399694   q,p=map(P,a[-2:])
%o A399694   x=next(x for x in count(1) if x not in a and len(s:=P(x))==2 and s&p and not s&q and s-p)
%o A399694   a+=x,
%o A399694  return a
%o A399694 print(EW2(100))
%Y A399694 Cf. A336957 (Enots-Wolley sequence), A007774 (numbers divisible by exactly two distinct primes), A001221 (number of distinct prime factors).
%K A399694 nonn,new
